WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.955
FedRAMP baselines define security control expectations for cloud service offerings based

00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:06.280
on impact and use case.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.910
They connect federal cloud risk to implementation, evidence, assessment,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.360
authorization support, and continuous monitoring.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.100
A baseline is not a marketing label and not a guarantee of perfect security.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.385
It is a structured starting point for demonstrating that a cloud service is protected and

00:00:20.385 --> 00:00:22.120
operated appropriately for federal use.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.840
Impact thinking is the foundation.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.520
Confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.330
and availability help teams understand what kind of harm could result from a security

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:29.780
incident.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.900
Low, tailored low-impact, Moderate,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.420
and High concepts help align security expectations with data sensitivity, mission impact,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.590
service complexity, and agency use.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.195
The right baseline should reflect system risk, not convenience or prestige.

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:46.280
Baselines are grounded in NIST control concepts but applied through the realities of cloud

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.000
architecture.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.640
Shared responsibility, inherited controls, customer responsibilities,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.975
control implementation statements, and evidence boundaries all matter.

00:00:53.975 --> 00:00:57.670
Two services under the same baseline can look different because their architectures,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.735
service models, dependencies, and operating processes are different.

00:01:00.735 --> 00:01:02.730
The baseline tells teams what to address;

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.000
the system design determines how it is addressed.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.245
The practical takeaway is simple: baseline work is operational work.

00:01:08.245 --> 00:01:12.695
It requires honest scope, real control implementation, assessable documentation,

00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:16.400
evidence from actual workflows, vulnerability management, incident readiness,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.620
change awareness, and continuous monitoring.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.195
The goal is not to collect paperwork.

00:01:20.195 --> 00:01:24.310
The goal is to help agencies make informed risk decisions about cloud services that are

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:24.930
documented,

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.000
tested, monitored, and operated for their federal mission context.
